\chapter{Archon target ABI and native driver}
\label{ch:84}
\index{Archon target ABI and native driver}
\index{MIR!Archon backend}

Archon emits target-pinned assembly for a verified MIR subset. The documented native build uses the Linux AMD64 SysV profile, a matching generated package, scalar ABI support, runtime archives, and an explicit assembler and linker. The optional SDK hook selects an exact target triple. The standalone path can also be built without the extension SDK.

\section{Pin the entire native path}
Record the verified MIR input, target manifest, assembly tool, linkage options and ABI archive identity. Publish assembly separately first when diagnosing a target issue. After linking, test signed arithmetic boundaries and a supported tuple or block-argument case against the reference MIR evaluator. A mismatch can originate in register/slot lowering, a calling convention, or runtime code. A different architecture label in a local configuration cannot create a matching generated package. Keep native results scoped to the provider and target you actually ran.

\section{Account for an invisible parameter}
Consider a generated function returning a tuple. Its caller reserves a
contiguous result area and passes the address as the first argument.
The native callee fills the area and returns void at this boundary.
Treating the function as a C routine returning a struct by value is
therefore not a portable substitute for the documented internal convention.
In particular, adding up only the source-visible parameters can put a
call on the wrong side of the generated package's limit.

In the tested package the parameter ceiling is 128. For an otherwise
eligible tuple-returning call with 127 ordinary scalar parameters, the
hidden pointer brings the count to 128. With 128 ordinary parameters
the count is 129 and generation must reject the call. Contrast a scalar
result: it does not require that result pointer. Testing only functions
whose arguments all fit the first few registers will never reach either
the stack-argument boundary or this accounting error.

For a data check put a negative floating zero in a tuple field beside a
high-bit unsigned value. The scalar ABI carries a binary32 value in the
low 32 bits of a word; it does not convert the number to an integer by
rounding. Verify the exact result words in caller storage and put canaries
beside them. Testing numerical equality with zero cannot detect loss of
its sign, and checking the fields alone would miss a write just past the
last field. These observations qualify a particular generated caller/callee
boundary; they do not describe a general aggregate foreign calling convention.

